 "pref": "A fit patient within 12 weeks of resection, with a total systemic-therapy duration of about 6 months. ESPAC-3 periampullary is the only phase III randomised trial to enrol a substantial ampullary population: 434 randomised and 428 analysed from 100 centres in 18 countries, of whom 297 had ampullary, 96 intrapancreatic bile duct and 35 other cancers (10 duodenal, 25 nondescript), allocated to observation (144), bolus fluorouracil with folinic acid (143) or gemcitabine (141). NCCN carries a category 1 designation for fluorouracil–leucovorin and for gemcitabine on the strength of it.",
 "avoid": "The primary analysis was negative. Median overall survival was 35.2 months (95% CI 27.2–43.0) with observation against 43.1 months (34.0–56.0) with chemotherapy, hazard ratio 0.86 (95% CI 0.66–1.11), χ²=1.33, P=0.25, and the trial did not meet its primary endpoint. Significance appeared only in the prespecified multivariable analysis adjusting for age, bile duct cancer, poor tumour differentiation and positive lymph nodes: hazard ratio 0.75 (95% CI 0.57–0.98), Wald χ²=4.53, P=0.03, in a model of 415 patients and 236 deaths. By arm, unadjusted, fluorouracil against observation gave 0.95 (0.71–1.28), P=0.74 and gemcitabine 0.77 (0.57–1.05), P=0.10, with a three-group log-rank χ² of 2.96, P=0.23. The trial was open-label, a fifth of the fluorouracil arm (28 of 143) never received a dose, and only about half of each chemotherapy arm completed six cycles. Serious treatment-related adverse events were more frequent with fluorouracil–folinic acid than gemcitabine (70 of 143 patients, 49%, reporting 162 events against 43 of 141, 30%, reporting 81; P=0.002), with grade 3–4 diarrhoea 14% against 4% and stomatitis 11% against 0%. The authors themselves describe the positive multivariable result and the gemcitabine-specific result as hypothesis generating.",
 "note": "Downgraded from B to C on the full-text read, and the reason is specific: this page cannot cite ESPAC-3 as establishing adjuvant benefit in ampullary cancer. The trial was powered on 243 deaths and 430 patients to detect a 12 percentage-point absolute gain in 5-year survival (hazard ratio 0.68) across the pooled periampullary population, and the authors state that a separate survival analysis for each tumour type could not be built into the design because the incidence of each would have required an unattainable sample. It was never powered for the ampullary subgroup, and no hypothesis test for ampullary-only survival is reported anywhere in the paper: within the 297 ampullary patients the medians are observation 40.6 months (95% CI 30.6–61.4, 59 deaths), fluorouracil 57.8 months (32.8–84.0, 50 deaths) and gemcitabine 70.8 months (45.3–not estimable, 42 deaths), and they appear without a P value. Two further features of the full text matter. First, the adjustment that turns the result positive was for four variables on two of which the observation arm was prognostically the worst at baseline — poorly differentiated 33% against 22% and 19%, node positive 62% against 60% and 54% — so the shift from 0.86 to 0.75 is partly correcting a randomisation imbalance rather than independently confirming the primary analysis, and its interval only just excludes 1. Second, pooling created a group in which chemotherapy looked harmful: in the 96 intrapancreatic bile duct patients, median survival was 27.2 months (15.4–31.9) with observation against 18.3 (12.9–28.7) with fluorouracil and 19.5 (16.2–36.1) with gemcitabine. Any pooled benefit is therefore carried by the ampullary and other patients, in whom it was never formally tested. Survival differed sharply by tumour type — ampullary 53.1 months (42.3–72.5), other 32.6, bile duct 20.9 (17.0–27.5), χ²=27.75, P<0.001, bile duct hazard ratio 2.13 (1.61–2.83) against ampullary — and the trial's own discussion concedes that intrapancreatic bile duct cancer may need to be treated as a separate entity. Observation-arm survival of 35 months is also far better than the ampullary literature of the era, which implies a fit, early-stage, selected population; screening data on who was excluded and why were not collected. See <i>nonpm</i> for a discrepancy in the extracted guideline text at this point."
