 "t": "ESPAC-3 was never powered for its ampullary subgroup and reports no hypothesis test for ampullary-only survival anywhere, so this page cannot cite it as establishing adjuvant benefit in ampullary cancer.",
 "why": "This is the single most important correction the full-text read produced. The trial was powered on 243 deaths and 430 patients to detect a 12 percentage-point absolute 5-year gain across the pooled periampullary population, and its authors state that a separate analysis for each tumour type could not be built into the design. Within the 297 ampullary patients the medians are observation 40.6 months (95% CI 30.6–61.4, 59 deaths), fluorouracil 57.8 months (32.8–84.0, 50 deaths) and gemcitabine 70.8 months (45.3–not estimable, 42 deaths), and they appear with no P value because none was computed. The primary analysis across all 428 patients was negative (hazard ratio 0.86, 95% CI 0.66–1.11, P=0.25) and became significant only after multivariable adjustment (0.75, 0.57–0.98, P=0.03) for age, bile duct histology, poor differentiation and node positivity — and the observation arm was prognostically the worst at baseline on two of those four (poorly differentiated 33% against 22% and 19%; node positive 62% against 60% and 54%), so the shift partly corrects a randomisation imbalance rather than independently confirming the result. Quoting the ampullary medians alone is the most favourable possible framing of this trial, and the page says so wherever it quotes them. Adjuvant chemotherapy is still offered, because the adjusted estimate and the registry data point the same way and because there is nothing better; it is offered as a reasonable choice, not as a demonstrated benefit."
