 "t": "ESPAC-3 was never powered for its ampullary subgroup and reports no hypothesis test for ampullary-only survival anywhere, so this page cannot cite it as establishing adjuvant benefit in ampullary cancer.",
 "why": "This is the single most important correction the full-text read produced. The trial was powered on 243 deaths and 430 patients to detect a 12 percentage-point absolute 5-year gain across the pooled periampullary population, and its authors state that a separate analysis for each tumour type could not be built into the design. Within the 297 ampullary patients the medians are observation 40.6 months (95% CI 30.6–61.4, 59 deaths), fluorouracil 57.8 months (32.8–84.0, 50 deaths) and gemcitabine 70.8 months (45.3–not estimable, 42 deaths), and they appear with no P value because none was computed. The primary analysis across all 428 patients was negative (hazard ratio 0.86, 95% CI 0.66–1.11, P=0.25) and became significant only after multivariable adjustment (0.75, 0.57–0.98, P=0.03) for age, bile duct histology, poor differentiation and node positivity — but observation had fewer poorly differentiated tumors (19% versus 33% with fluorouracil/folinic acid and 22% with gemcitabine) and fewer positive nodes (54% versus 62% and 60%). Observation had more positive margins (19% versus 14% and 15%). These mixed baseline differences do not support a global prognostic ranking of the arms, and the adjusted analysis is not an independent confirmation of the primary result. Quoting the ampullary medians alone is the most favourable possible framing of this trial, and the page says so wherever it quotes them. Adjuvant chemotherapy is still offered, because the adjusted estimate and the registry data point the same way and because there is nothing better; it is offered as a reasonable choice, not as a demonstrated benefit."
